-- title:
--   Degree-zero functions on the two standard charts of a Weierstrass model
-- statement:
--   Let $R$ be a commutative ring and let $V$ be a projective Weierstrass cubic over $R$ (`WeierstrassCurve.Projective R`). Write $A = \mathrm{MvPolynomial}\,(\mathrm{Fin}\,3)\,R / (V.\mathrm{polynomial})$ for the quotient of the polynomial ring in three variables by the ideal generated by the Weierstrass cubic, graded by $\mathcal{A}_i =$ the image of the $R$-submodule of degree-$i$ homogeneous polynomials under the quotient map; this is `projModelGradingCR V`. Let $Y$ and $Z$ be the classes of the variables $X_1$ and $X_2$, which lie in $\mathcal{A}_1$ because $X_1$ and $X_2$ are homogeneous of degree $1$. The assertion is the conjunction of two statements. First, the ring homomorphism $\mathcal{A}_0 \to \mathrm{Away}\,\mathcal{A}\,Y$ sending $r$ to $r/1$ into the degree-zero part of the homogeneous localisation at the powers of $Y$ is injective. Second, for every $a$ in $\mathrm{Away}\,\mathcal{A}\,Y$ and every $b$ in $\mathrm{Away}\,\mathcal{A}\,Z$ whose images in $\mathrm{Away}\,\mathcal{A}\,(Y\cdot Z)$ under the two transition maps `awayMap` agree, there is an element $r$ of $\mathcal{A}_0$ with $r/1 = a$ in $\mathrm{Away}\,\mathcal{A}\,Y$ and $r/1 = b$ in $\mathrm{Away}\,\mathcal{A}\,Z$. Injectivity is stated only at $Y$; the corresponding statement at $Z$ follows by symmetry.
--
--   This is the algebraic heart of the computation of the global functions on the projective Weierstrass model over an arbitrary base: the two standard affine charts $D_+(Y)$ and $D_+(Z)$ cover the model away from nothing relevant, and sections agreeing on the overlap come from the degree-zero part of the homogeneous coordinate ring, which is $R$. It is used in the proof that the structure morphism of the projective Weierstrass model induces a bijection on top-level sections after base change ([`WeierstrassProjModel.bijective_appTop_pullback_snd_projModelStrCR`](thm.html#WeierstrassProjModel.bijective_appTop_pullback_snd_projModelStrCR)).
